# Untitled

Realizing Stable Perovskite Solar Cells with Efficiency Exceeding 25.6% Through Crystallization Kinetics and Spatial Orientation Regulation
Organic-inorganic hybrid perovskites have emerged as highly promising candidates for photovoltaic applications, owing to the exceptional optoelectronic properties and low cost. Nonetheless, the performance and stability of solar cells suffer from the defect states of perovskite films aroused by non-optically active phases and non-centralized crystal orientation. Herein, a versatile organic molecule, Hydantoin, to modulate the crystallization of perovskite, is developed. Benefiting from the diverse functional groups, more spatially oriented perovskite films with high crystallinity are formed. This enhancement is accompanied by a conspicuous reduction in defect density, yielding efficiency of \(25.66\%\) (certified \(25.15\%\)), with superb environmental stability. Notably, under the standard measurement conditions (ISOS-L-11), the maximum power point (MPP) output maintains \(96.8\%\) of the initial efficiency for \(1600\,\mathrm{h}\) and exhibits excellent ion migration suppression. The synergistic regulation of crystallization and spatial orientation offers novel avenues for propelling perovskite solar cell (PSC) development.
## 1. Introduction
Halide perovskite materials have emerged as promising contenders for photovoltaic applications, due to their exceptional
optoelectronic properties and cost-effective solution processing techniques.[1] Over the past ten years, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has rapidly escalated, soaring from \(3.8\%^{[2]}\) to over \(26\%^{[3,4]}\) rivaling crystalline silicon technology. Nevertheless, to achieve the commercialization of PSCs, excellent PCE, stability, and large-scale fabrication should be realized simultaneously.[5] Control over the crystallization process of perovskites is the key to achieve the above-mentioned targets.[6] However, the crystallization process of organic-inorganic hybrid perovskites is highly intricate.[7] The involvement of intermediate phases, such as the mixed-solvent phase and the \(\delta\)-phase, renders it challenging to achieve a homogenous perovskite film,[8] which subsequently results in significant lattice distortions, random orientations, and trapping centers.[9]
Crystallization regulation[10] has proven to be an effective method to enhance the quality of perovskite films and devices performance. The crystallization process of perovskite typically commences from the Pb-I framework as it forms nano-scale nucleation centers in the precursor solution. Consequently, efforts have primarily concentrated on introducing additives into the perovskite precursor to modify the Pb-I structure for better control.[11] Organic additives with \(-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\), \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), and imidazole groups have been utilized to optimize the phase transition and crystallization of perovskite by forming \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-additive adduct clusters.[12] For instance, through \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-NMP adduct-controlled crystallization,[10] high PCEs up to \(23\%\) for small-area PSCs and surpassing \(20\%\) for miniature modules have been attained. The robust coordination capability between \(-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}/-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) also plays a pivotal role in facilitating the formation of perovskite phases and bolstering device performance. Utilizing methyl carbamate (MAFa)[13] as a synergistic agent in the crystallization process of FA perovskite effectively mitigates the emergence of detrimental intermediate phases, thereby facilitating the formation of a stable black phase at temperatures below the phase transition point. This approach yields a noteworthy PCE surpassing \(24\%\), while also attaining exceptional environmental stability. The imidazole derivative, 1-carboxymethyl-3-methylimidazolium chloride (ImAcCl),[14] exercises control over crystallization by
preferentially selecting the growth of crystalline phases, thus further amplifying the photovoltaic performance.
Beyond coordination-induced crystallization control, passivation of defects at interfaces[15] and grain boundaries[16] constitutes another essential approach toward enhancing cell PCE and stability. Moreover, photoactive phases oriented out-of-plane exhibit greater propensity for carrier transport than randomly oriented ones. Leveraging organic long-chain ammonium halides such as phenylethylammonium iodide (PEAI)[17] and butylammonium iodide (BAI)[18] for control enables the formation of 2D/2D-3D structured perovskite with more concentrated out-of-plane orientation. The employment of pentanamidine hydrochloride (PAD)-assisted crystallization[19] facilitates a more concentrated spatial out-of-plane orientation of the photoactive phase of perovskite during the nucleation stage preceding annealing, further boosting the PCE to over \(25\%\). If the effect of Lewis basic organic small molecules in promoting the growth of photoactive phases, regulating more concentrated out-of-plane orientation, and efficiently passivating defects can be achieved synergistically, it will be a tremendous advancement for simplifying the processing complexity and improving the photoelectric performance of efficient and stable PSCs.
Herein, we report the attainment of highly efficient and stable PSCs through the utilization of an imidazole derivative, Hydantoin, as an additive. The \(-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{C}\) and \(-\mathrm{NH}\) groups present in Hydantoin effectively coordinate with \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)ions, respectively. This coordination significantly enhances the crystallization of the Pb-I framework and facilitates the growth of perovskite crystals. Notably, Hydantoin exerts control over the photoactive phase of the perovskite, promoting a more concentrated out-of-plane orientation effect. Furthermore, Hydantoin distributes across the surface and grain boundaries of films, effectively passivating defects and stabilizing the surface electronic structure. Through the synergistic action of auxiliary crystallization and orientational regulation, the PCE of small-area PSCs has been elevated to \(25.66\%\) (certified \(25.15\%\)). Compared with the control PSCs the Hydantoin-based-PSC exhibits much better operational stability, maintaining \(96.8\%\) of its initial power output after continuous operation for 1600 hs under maximum power point (MPP) under continuous light illumination (ISOS-L-II protocol).[20] Significantly, these multifunctional additive curbs halogen anion migration in PSCs, ensuring greater stability in performance. Overall, the synergistic impact of our multifunctional additive on auxiliary crystallization and orientation regulation offers a highly viable pathway toward advancing the development and commercialization of PSC.
## 2. Results and Discussion
To detect the ability of Hydantoin to regulate the crystallization process of perovskite materials, synchrotron-based in-situ grazing incidence wide-angle x-ray scattering (GIWAXS) measurements were performed during the spin coating and annealing process \((150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) of perovskite, both without and with Hydantoin (Figure 1a,b). The x-ray incident angle was set to \(2^{\circ}\) to perform full-depth diffraction. As shown in Figures S1 and S3 (Supporting Information), the perovskite film with Hydantoin (PVK-Hydantoin) forms significant \(\alpha\) -phase perovskite after \(5\ s\) of the spin coating process, and the formation of intermediate phases \((\mathrm{PbI}_{2}@\mathrm{DMF}:\mathrm{DMSO},\,\mathrm{q}=0.70\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\), DMF: N,N-Dimethylformamide, DMSO: Dimethyl sulfoxide)[13] is remarkably suppressed. As shown in Figures S2 and S3 (Supporting Information), there are many \(\delta\) -phase perovskites \((\mathrm{q}=0.86\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})\)[21] in the pristine film at the initial stage of the annealing process, which are significantly suppressed in the PVK-Hydantoin film. On the other hand, the pristine film decomposes and produces lead iodide at \(\approx2\ \mathrm{min}\) when annealing in air atmosphere, while the PVK-Hydantoin film shows decomposition at \(\approx5\ \mathrm{min}\). This phenomenon indicates that Hydantoin can significantly improve the stability of perovskite films toward humidity and heat. This is probably related to the reduction of attack sites for water molecules and the reduction of perovskite grain boundaries by Hydantoin. Integration of the perovskite (100) crystal orientation \((\mathrm{q}=1\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})\)[22] from in situ 2D-GIWAXS with different spatial orientation angles, as shown in Figure 1c–f. The smaller values of \(\chi\) angle indicate a stronger tendency toward out-of-plane orientation, perpendicular to the film plane. Notably, the (100) crystal orientation of PVK-Hydantoin film exhibits a higher degree of out-of-plane orientation after annealing, while the (100) crystal orientation of pristine perovskite film displays a random spatial orientation. This orientation process of the PVK-Hydantoin film during the annealing occurs gradually (Figure 1e,f), rather than instantaneously, and lasts for \(\approx60\ s\). Density functional theory (DFT) calculations demonstrate that Hydantoin has a stronger binding affinity with the (100) crystallographic plane of perovskite (Figure S15, Supporting Information), leading it to preferentially bind to the (100) crystallographic plane to reduce its surface energy. This action promotes the formation of the black phase perovskite and modulates its spatially concentrated orientation.[23] The out-of-plane oriented (100) crystal direction of perovskite is beneficial for the vertical transport of carriers in the films.[24]
