As shown in the schematic diagram of Figure 1g, during the rapid diffusion and spin coating stages (Stage I and Stage II) of the organic ammonium halide solution in the precursor \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) films containing Hydantoin, a large amount of black-phase perovskite is formed, and the formation of \(\delta\) -phase perovskite and intermediate phase related to \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) is significantly suppressed. Subsequently, during the annealing process, the evolution of the out-of-plane orientation of \(\alpha\) -phase perovskite is completed, ultimately resulting in the formation of a PVK-Hydantoin film with higher crystallinity. With the increase of Hydantoin addition up to \(1\ \mathrm{mg\,mL^{-1}}\), the diffraction intensity of the perovskite (100) crystal plane enhances, and the unreacted precursor \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) decreases significantly (Figure S4, Supporting Information). Benefiting from the effective crystallization regulation of Hydantoin, the grain size of PVK-Hydantoin film increases significantly, and the number of grain boundaries decreases (Figure 1h,i; Figure S5, Supporting Information). The different contrast grains located at the grain boundary are probably excess \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\).[25] There is no significant difference in the light absorption ability between pristine PVK films and PVK-Hydantoin film (Figure S6, Supporting Information).
To investigate the mechanism of Hydantoin in regulating perovskite crystallization, we initially studied the interaction between the precursor \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and Hydantoin. Pure \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and a mixture of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and Hydantoin were added to pure DMF solution, respectively. The solutions were then heated to \(70\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with
stirring for \(30~\mathrm{s}\) (Figure 2a,b), as well as heated to \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 1 min with stirring (Figure S7b, Supporting Information). The solutes in the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-Hydantoin solution demonstrated enhanced solubility. After heating at \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) and stirring for \(30~\mathrm{s}\), all \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) was completely dissolved (Figure S7c, Supporting Information). Subsequently the solution was cooled to \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) (Figure 2c,d; Figure S7d, Supporting Information). It was observed that a substantial amount of solute precipitated out of the pristine \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) solution, while the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-Hydantoin solution remained clear. The precipitation of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) is attributed to the gradual crystallization of the supersaturated solution upon temperature reduction. This finding suggests the strong coordination effect between \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and Hydantoin. As depicted in Figure 2e and Figure S8a (Supporting Information), the addition of Hydantoin leads to a significant reduction in the content of the intermediate phase \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}@\mathrm{DMF}\):DMSO within the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-Hydantoin film \((\mathrm{q}=0.70\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})\), as well as a decrease in the crystallinity of
the (100) crystal plane. This observation was further confirmed by X-ray diffraction analysis (XRD) (Figure S8b, Supporting Information). High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) and electron diffraction images (Figure S9, Supporting Information) show that the presence of hydantoin-induced morphology change of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) grains at the nanoscale. The \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-Hydantoin film, which was hindered from crystallization by Hydantoin, exhibited an increase in the number of pinholes per unit area, facilitating the subsequent diffusion of the amine salt solution, as shown in the SEM images (Figure S10, Supporting Information).
The mechanism underlying the interaction between Hydantoin and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) has been elucidated through quantum chemical calculations and the Interacting Region Indicator (IRI) method, as depicted in Figure 2f. In this representation, blue signifies attraction, while red denotes repulsion between atoms/ions. The unique molecular configuration of Hydantoin confers it with
exceptionally high binding energy, enabling a single Hydantoin molecule to simultaneously bind to two \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) molecules with an energy of up to \(1.798\,\mathrm{eV}\). Specifically, the \(-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) group in the Hydantoin molecule binds to the \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) ion of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\), while the \(-\mathrm{NH}\) group forms hydrogen bonds with the \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)ion in \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\). This proposed mechanism is further supported by the shifts observed in the liquid-state \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) and liquid-state \({}^{13}\mathrm{C}\) NMR spectral peaks of pure Hydantoin and a mixture of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and Hydantoin, as presented in Figure S11 (Supporting Information). Additionally, Fourier Transform Infrared (FTIR) spectra of the pristine \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) film and the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)-Hydantoin film exhibited a blue shift in the characteristic peak positions of \(-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) and \(-\mathrm{NH}\) (Figure 2g–i), further corroborating the strong interaction between Hydantoin and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\). X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS) analysis further revealed a lower binding energy shift in the characteristic peaks of Pb 4f and I 3d (Figure S12, Supporting Information) and a lower binding energy shift in the characteristic peaks of O 1s (Figure S13a, Supporting Information), providing additional evidence for the strong coordination ability of Hydantoin with \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) in the solid film state.[26] Based on
the results obtained from FTIR and XPS analysis, which revealed prominent functional group signals associated with Hydantoin, and no new diffraction peaks or peak shifts were observed in the XRD tests, it can be inferred that Hydantoin is present at the grain boundaries and surfaces of the perovskite material, but does not infiltrate into the perovskite lattice structure.
To further substantiate the binding effect between Hydantoin and perovskite, and ascertain its defect passivation capability, FTIR spectroscopy was conducted on both the pristine perovskite film and the PVK-Hydantoin film (Figure 2g–i). The results indicated a decrease in wavenumber for the characteristic peaks associated with \(-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) and \(-\mathrm{NH}\),[27] indicating a strong interaction between Hydantoin and perovskite. Additionally, XPS analysis demonstrated a shift in the characteristic peaks of Pb 4f and I 3d toward lower binding energy (Figure 2j,k), while the characteristic peaks of O 1s shifted toward higher binding energy (Figure S13a, Supporting Information). These findings provide compelling evidence for effective chemical coordination and hydrogen bonding between perovskite and Hydantoin.[28] The XPS (N1s) spectrum shown in Figure S13b (Supporting
Information) exhibits the peak of the functional group \(-\mathrm{NH}\) in Hydantoin. The peak position of \(-\mathrm{NH}\) shifted from \(399.87~\mathrm{eV}\) (Hydantoin) to \(400.24~\mathrm{eV}\) \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) -Hydantoin), indicating the interaction of hydrogen bonding between \(-\mathrm{NH}\) and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) . Notably, the XPS Pb 4f spectrum data of PVK-Hydantoin revealed a significant reduction in the formation of deep-level defects \(\mathrm{Pb}_{0}\) , further corroborating the passivation ability of Hydantoin. The XPS full scan is presented in Figure S13c (Supporting Information).
The total density of states (tDOS) spectrum (Figure 3a), obtained through Mott-Schottky analysis (Figure S14a, Supporting Information) and Capacitance-frequency (C-f) tests (Figure S14b, Supporting Information), was utilized to evaluate the defect passivation efficacy of Hydantoin. The results unequivocally demonstrate the exceptional ability of Hydantoin for passivating defects, with the density of deep and shallow level defects in PSCs-Hydantoin films significantly reduced by several-fold compared to the pristine PSCs. Furthermore, the IRI image obtained from DFT calculations (Figure S16, Supporting Information) elucidates the binding mechanisms of Hydantoin with perovskite crystals, highlighting the interaction of \(-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) with incompletely coordinated \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) ions and the formation of hydrogen bonds between \(-\mathrm{NH}\) and incompletely coordinated \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)ions. The binding energy of Hydantoin with the periodic perovskite crystal, as obtained through DFT simulation, is \(-0.923\;\mathrm{eV}.\) Additionally, the Differential charge density images provides compelling evidence for the strong coupling effect and significant charge transfer between Hydantoin and perovskite (Figure 3b), indicating that electrons tend to move toward the perovskite.[29] Given that organic-inorganic hybrid perovskite is an ionic semiconductor, ion migration occurs under illumination and bias, with positively charged iodine vacancies serving as carrier trapping centers. DFT calculations further reveal that the binding energy of Hydantoin with perovskite crystals containing iodine vacancies is \(-0.66~\mathrm{eV}\) (Figure 3c; Figure S17, Supporting Information). The corresponding structural imagery demonstrates that the two
