\(-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) groups in Hydantoin can effectively bind with the undercoordinated \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) ions near the iodine vacancies. Therefore, the Lewis basic functional group in Hydantoin enables it to actively fill carrier defects, thereby effectively inhibiting carrier recombination at grain boundaries and interfaces, which could enhance carrier extraction efficiency of PSCs. This beneficial effect of Hydantoin could improve the overall performance of PSCs by reducing energy losses due to carrier recombination and increasing the collection of photogenerated carriers.[30] To investigate the conductive properties of the films, Conductive Atomic Force Microscopy (c-AFM) measurements were performed under the same bias voltage. The conductivity of the PVK-Hydantoin film was found to be enhanced, which can be attributed to the improved crystallinity of the (100) perovskite phase and reduced defect density. Additionally, AFM coupled with Kelvin Probe Force Microscopy (KPFM) was utilized to examine the influence of Hydantoin on surface morphology and surface potential (Figure S18, Supporting Information; Figure 3e). KPFM images demonstrate that the surface potential of the PVK-Hydantoin film is superior to that of the pristine PVK film, exhibiting a more concentrated distribution. This characteristic facilitates more efficient hole extraction,[15,31] which can be attributed to the enhanced presence of the (100) perovskite phase.[32] The results indicate that the surface of PVK-Hydantoin film demonstrated a higher electrochemical potential compared to the pristine film. The steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and electroluminescence (EL) measurements of the single-layer perovskite films reveal that the PVK-Hydantoin film exhibits a fluorescence intensity twice as high as that of the pristine perovskite films, as shown in Figure S19a,c (Supporting Information). The Time-Resolved Photoluminescence (TRPL) results depicted in Figure S19c (Supporting Information) further illustrate that the carrier lifetime of the PVK-Hydantoin film has increased from 335.9 nanoseconds (ns) to 802.2 ns. This enhancement confirms that Hydantoin effectively minimizes non-radiative recombination in the perovskite film. For further details, the pertinent parameters are presented in Table S3 (Supporting Information). Transient photovoltage (TPV) and transient photocurrent (TPC) techniques are commonly used to study carrier dynamics in solar cells. When solar cells are irradiated under pulse light conditions at open-circuit voltage, photogenerated electrons and holes are generated, resulting in the formation of a photovoltage. After the pulse light is turned off, the photovoltage gradually decreases over time due to carrier recombination. The longer T PV decay observed in PSC with Hydantoin (PSC-Hydantoin) typically indicates slower charge carrier recombination in the PSCs (Figure S19e, Supporting Information), which can be attributed to its out-of-plane orientation and lower defect state density.[33] In contrast, the faster TPC decay represents more efficient carrier transport and extraction in PSC-Hydantoin device[34] (Figure S19d, Supporting Information). To quantify the total defect state density within perovskite films, we fabricated electron-only Space charge limited current (SCLC) devices and hole-only SCLC devices (Figure S20, Supporting Information). The introduction of Hydantoin significantly reduced the total electron defect state density to \(39.09\%\) and the total hole defect state density to \(55.76\%\) of the pristine devices. We conducted a thorough investigation into the dependence of \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) on light intensity (Figure S21a,b, Supporting Information) to gain insight into the recombination mechanisms. Additionally,
trap-assisted non-radiative recombination causes the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\)-light intensity slope to approach \(2\mathrm{KT/q}\), where K is the Boltzmann constant, T is the temperature, and q is the elementary charge. The lower fitting slope observed in PSC-Hydantoin devices compared to the pristine devices \((1.378\;\mathrm{KT/q}\) versus \(1.812\;\mathrm{KT/q})\) implies a reduced proportion of trap-assisted non-radiative recombination following Hydantoin passivation.[35] To further verify the ability of Hydantoin to suppress carrier recombination, we conducted selected area PL mapping on both pristine perovskite and PVK-Hydantoin films, examining the fluorescence lifetime and related PL intensity on a microscale (Figure 3f,g). The results indicate that the PL intensity and carrier lifetime intensity of PVK-Hydantoin film are higher than those of pristine perovskite film, with this phenomenon persisting at grain boundaries. In summary, our findings demonstrate that Hydantoin effectively passivates grain boundaries and surface defects in perovskite films, leading to suppressed non-radiative carrier recombination and improved carrier lifetime.
PSCs were fabricated with the addition of Hydantoin, the cross-sectional structure of devices was depicted in Figure 4a, with layers consisting of conductive glass \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)/perovskite/Spiro-OMeTA/D/Au from bottom to top. The statistical data of the performance of PSCs devices, tested under standard AM 1.5G 1 sun illumination, are summarized in Figure 4b, Figure S21 and Table S1 (Supporting Information). The average PCE of the PSCs-Hydantoin is \(25.13\%\), significantly higher than that of the pristine PSCs \((23.79\%)\). The champion PCE achieved through Hydantoin regulation in the PSC is \(25.66\%\), with \(J_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(26.184\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-1}\). \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(1.167\;\mathrm{V}\) and \(FF\) of \(83.96\%\). The PCE of the third-party certified PSC-Hydantoin device (with an effective area of \(0.1\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\)) reaches \(25.15\%\) (Figures S27 and S28, Supporting Information). Evidently, crystallization regulation and defects passivation greatly enhance the photovoltaic performance of PSCs. Figure 4b,c shows the characteristics of the current density-voltage \((J\!\!\cdot\!\!V)\) curves of the champion PSCs devices. As demonstrated in Figure S22 and Table S2 (Supporting Information), the PCE increases with the rising Hydantoin concentration in the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) precursor solution, attaining its peak value at the concentration of \(1\;\mathrm{mg\;mL^{-1}}\). The external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of Pristine PSC and PSC-Hydantoin are shown in Figure 4e. Additionally, we measured the relationship between PCE based on the MPP and time (Figure 4f) to evaluate the stable output performance of the pristine PSC and PSC-Hydantoin. The slower decay rate indicates that Hydantoin significantly improves the operational stability of PSC.
In the \(J\!\!\cdot\!\!V\) tests of PSCs devices, it was observed that Hydantoin can significantly mitigate the hysteresis effect of the devices, often associated with ion migration.[36] Therefore, Auger electron spectroscopy (AES) was utilized to characterize the ion migration in PSCs devices. The AES element distribution profile of PSCs after continuous output at the MPP under 1 sun illumination for \(3\;\mathrm{h}\) are presented in Figure 5a,b. The results demonstrate that after \(3\;\mathrm{h}\) of continuous operation, the pristine PSC device exhibited a significant higher I element signal relative to the PSC-Hydantoin in the Au electrode layer and the HTL layer. The result was in good agreement with energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) scanning, which was conducted on the I element signal on the Au electrode surface of PSCs devices before and after
continuous operation at MPP for \(3\,\mathrm{~h~}\) (Figure S23, Supporting Information). The findings reveal that the I element signal on the Au electrode surface of the PSC-Hydantoin device remained largely unchanged, while the signal collected from the pristine PSC device notably augmented after \(3\,\mathrm{~h~}\) of continuous illumination and output. Additionally, dark \(J_{-}V\) measurements were obtained for pristine PSC and PSC-Hydantoin devices following continuous output at MPP for 0s, 300s, 800s, and 1800s. The results indicate that the curve offset of the PSC-Hydantoin device decreased by half compared to the pristine PSC device (Figure S24a,b, Supporting Information). EL tests conducted before and after continuous output at MPP for \(90\ \mathrm{min}\) revealed a pronounced shift in the spectra of the pristine PSC device (Figure S24c, Supporting Information), whereas this phenomenon was not evident in the tests of the PSC-Hydantoin device. Based on the above results, we propose that Hydantoin efficiently impedes ion migration in PSCs devices. This can be ascribed to the proactive filling of charged defects in perovskite films by Hydantoin, as well as the formation of hydrogen bonds between Hydantoin and \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)ions, which serve to stabilize the \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\)ions within the perovskite materials.[37] The XRD evolution of both pristine PVK films and PVK-Hydantoin films, stored in an environment of \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(60-\) \(70\%\) humidity air for \(360\,\mathrm{~h~}\), is depicted in Figure 5c and Figure S25 (Supporting Information), respectively. The findings reveal a notable amplification of the \(\delta\)-phase and a substantial diminution of the \(\alpha\)-phase in the pristine PVK film after being subjected to humidity aging for \(360\,\mathrm{~h~}\). On the other hand, the PVK-Hydantoin film manifests superior moisture stability. The photographs and ultraviolet and visible spectra (UV–vis) of pristine perovskite and PVK-Hydantoin films, before and after storage in saturated humidity air \((25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) for \(72\,\mathrm{~h~}\), are presented in Figure 5d, demonstrating remarkable high-humidity stability of the PVK-Hydantoin film. Under identical aging conditions as the aforementioned test, severe decay of surface grains has been observed on the pristine perovskite film (Figure 5e; Figure S26a, Supporting Information), accompanied by blurred grain boundaries. In contrast, the PVK-Hydantoin film maintains a clear distinction of grain boundaries (Figure 5f; Figure S26b, Supporting Information). To explore the stability-enhancing effect of Hydantoin on PSCs devices, pristine PSCs and PSCs-Hydantoin devices were stored in air with a humidity of \(30{-}40\%\) \((25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) based on the standard measurement condition ISOS-D-1.[20] PSCs-Hydantoin devices retained \(80.9\%\) of their initial PCE, while the pristine PSCs devices diminished to \(44.6\%\) of their initial PCE (Figure 5g). Using the ISOS-L-1[20] standard measurement conditions, a comparison of the long-term intrinsic stability of the pristine PSC and PSC-Hydantoin was conducted (Figure 5h). Continuous operation tests were performed at the MPP under one-sun illumination conditions in a nitrogen atmosphere at room temperature. The results demonstrate that after \(1600\,\mathrm{~h~}\) of MPP output, the PSC-Hydantoin device still maintained over \(96.8\%\) of its initial PCE, whereas the pristine PSC device decayed to \(50.3\%\) of its initial PCE. Due to the passivation of defects resulting from the coating of Hydantoin on perovskite grains and grain boundaries, as well as the increased crystallinity and reduced grain boundaries achieved through crystallization regulation in PVK-Hydantoin films, the PVK-Hydantoin films exhibit a lower incidence of water and oxygen attack sites, thereby improving the humidity stability and working stability of PSCs-Hydantoin devices. Furthermore, the efficient suppression of ion migration by Hydantoin considerably enhances the continuous operating stability of PSCs.
